so let me give you a really really  simple example of a differential a  private technique for one task so  suppose you have a big lecture hall  filled with you know 500 students or  something and you want to know what  fraction of them cheated on at least one  exam during their first year so you want  to ask them about an Abba havior that's  not only embarrassing but probably a  violation of honor code and you could  give them the following instructions  it's if you know it's going to give them  a method of plausible deniability  basically so the instructions are as  follows the first flip a coin don't tell  me the outcome of the coin if the coin  came up heads answer the question  truthfully if the coin came up tails  flip another coin and if the second coin  came up heads say yes and otherwise say  no so you see that a student who never  cheated still has a 1/4 probability of  saying I cheated and a student who  cheated still has a 1/4 probability of  thing I didn't cheat and so you can  gather up now these basically randomized  responses but randomized with a bias  toward the truth randomize responses to  your query and you do a little bit of  reverse engineering and you can get a  close approximation to the true fraction  of students who cheated during at some  point during their first year assuming  they follow the instructions correctly  and that's called randomize response and  it's a technique that was developed in  1965 by Stanley Warner and differential  privacy actually assigns a formal  measure of privacy lost there that's  associated with the ratio of the  probable  of producing say the answer of yes when  the truth is yes versus when the truth  is no and it turns out that differential  privacy will tell you this is the  logarithm of three differentially  private that's our epsilon for that  procedure you could make it more privacy  protective by increasing the chances  that they will answer randomly and  decreasing the chances that they answer  truthfully by instead of starting with a  fair coin starting with a coin that's  biased toward telling them to answer  randomly and that oddly this thing  randomized response is fancy versions of  it that's what is being used by Apple  now in iOS 10 and also by Google and the  in for certain things in the Chrome  browser
 

